cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "tsp-lab: Beam-Guided Memetic Annealing (BGMA), benchmarked against known TSP optima"
abstract: >-
  A TSP laboratory in dependency-free JavaScript. It contains Beam-Guided
  Memetic Annealing (BGMA), a fixed-budget heuristic with a low/mid/high/xhigh/max
  effort ladder; an LK-style local search baseline; Christofides with blossom
  matching; a Held-Karp exact solver for n <= 22; a Held-Karp-style 1-tree lower
  bound; and a benchmark suite scoring every method against exact optima,
  published TSPLIB optima and the 1-tree bound. Measured results: every effort
  level matched the Held-Karp optimum on 26/26 instances at n = 18-22; at max
  effort BGMA returned the published optimum in 89 of 100 seeded runs on ten
  TSPLIB instances up to n = 442 (never on pcb442) and was +0.50% to +2.10%
  above it (median) at n = 574-1060. Given the same CPU time, restarts of the
  LK-style baseline match or beat BGMA max on all 14 TSPLIB instances. The
  1-tree bound is shown to overstate the true gap by up to 5.34 percentage
  points. See paper/main.pdf.
type: software
